WEBVTT

1
00:01:05.808 --> 00:01:08.059
You did a wonderful job. Wonderful.

2
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.520
Shh. Shh.

3
00:01:10.521 --> 00:01:12.021
We did it, Pete. We did it.

4
00:01:14.024 --> 00:01:15.817
- For a moment, we had our doubts.
- Shh.

5
00:01:15.818 --> 00:01:19.320
To continue, you've listened
to a long and complex case...

6
00:01:16.514 --> 00:01:18.807
Thank you very much, sir.

7
00:01:19.321 --> 00:01:21.197
murder in the first degree.

8
00:01:21.198 --> 00:01:26.244
A premeditated murder is the most serious
charge tried in our criminal courts.

9
00:01:26.245 --> 00:01:28.079
You've listened to the testimony.

10
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.082
You've had the law read to you and
interpreted as it applies in this case.

11
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:35.186
It's now your duty to sit down and try
and separate the facts from the fancy.

12
00:01:36.004 --> 00:01:39.649
One man is dead. Another
man's life is at stake.

13
00:01:40.342 --> 00:01:44.428
If there's a reasonable doubt in your
minds as to the guilt of the accused...

14
00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:46.430
a reasonable doubt...

15
00:01:46.431 --> 00:01:49.308
then you must bring me
a verdict of not guilty.

16
00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.269
Now if, however, there's
no reasonable doubt...

17
00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.247
then you must in good conscience
find the accused guilty.

18
00:01:56.275 --> 00:02:00.486
However you decide, your
verdict must be unanimous.

19
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:03.948
In the event that you find
the accused guilty...

20
00:02:03.949 --> 00:02:07.368
the bench will not entertain
a recommendation for mercy.

21
00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:10.663
The death sentence is
mandatory in this case.

22
00:02:10.664 --> 00:02:14.475
You're faced with a grave responsibility.
Thank you, gentlemen.

23
00:02:16.378 --> 00:02:18.521
The alternate jurors are excused.

24
00:02:24.428 --> 00:02:26.529
The jury will now retire.

25
00:04:24.423 --> 00:04:26.524
Try this one.
See if I can get it...

26
00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:29.610
It's a muggy day.

27
00:04:32.764 --> 00:04:35.224
Hey. Oh, oh.

28
00:04:47.195 --> 00:04:49.363
- Piece of gum?
- No, thanks.

29
00:04:49.364 --> 00:04:51.532
This thing is...

30
00:04:51.533 --> 00:04:53.259
- Huh?
- ...move it.

31
00:04:54.786 --> 00:04:57.038
- Come on, I'll give you a hand.
- Give me a hand with that.

32
00:04:57.039 --> 00:04:58.472
That's it.

33
00:05:04.004 --> 00:05:05.713
- You know something?
- Yeah?

34
00:05:05.714 --> 00:05:07.548
I called the weather
bureau this morning.

35
00:05:07.549 --> 00:05:09.550
This is gonna be the
hottest day of the year.

36
00:05:09.551 --> 00:05:11.218
Could be.

37
00:05:11.219 --> 00:05:13.220
Boy, you'd think they'd at least
air-condition these places.

38
00:05:13.221 --> 00:05:16.640
- What is your name, sir?
- Oh, it's, uh... That one, yeah.

39
00:05:16.641 --> 00:05:18.142
Thank you very much.

40
00:05:18.143 --> 00:05:20.978
Okay, gentlemen, everybody's here.

41
00:05:20.979 --> 00:05:23.898
Now, if there's anything you want,
I'll be right outside the door.

42
00:05:23.899 --> 00:05:26.250
- Just knock.
- Okay, thanks. We will.

43
00:05:38.663 --> 00:05:39.955
Good morning.

44
00:05:39.956 --> 00:05:41.499
I never knew they locked the door.

45
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.767
Sure they lock the door.
What'd you think?

46
00:05:44.753 --> 00:05:47.271
I don't know. It just
never occurred to me.

47
00:05:48.799 --> 00:05:50.674
What's that for?

48
00:05:50.675 --> 00:05:52.635
Oh, I thought we might
want to vote by ballot.

49
00:05:52.636 --> 00:05:55.387
Great idea. Maybe we can
get him elected senator.

50
00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.408
Just in case, you know.

51
00:06:02.813 --> 00:06:05.022
- How'd you like it?
- Oh, I don't know.

52
00:06:05.023 --> 00:06:06.774
It was pretty interesting.

53
00:06:06.775 --> 00:06:08.818
Yeah? I almost fell asleep.

54
00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:11.487
I mean, I've never
been on a jury before.

55
00:06:11.488 --> 00:06:14.298
No? I've sat on many juries.

56
00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:18.369
What gets me is the way those
lawyers talk and talk and talk...

57
00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.621
even when it's an open-and-shut
case like this one.

58
00:06:20.622 --> 00:06:22.414
Did you ever hear so
much talk about nothin'?

59
00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:24.375
Well, I guess they're entitled.

60
00:06:24.376 --> 00:06:26.936
Yeah. They're entitled.
It's the system, but...

61
00:06:28.046 --> 00:06:32.216
If you ask me, I'd slap those tough kids
down before they start any trouble.

62
00:06:32.217 --> 00:06:34.385
It would save us a lot
of time and money.

63
00:06:34.386 --> 00:06:36.011
- Hey, let's get started, huh?
- That's a good idea.

64
00:06:36.012 --> 00:06:38.722
Yeah, let's get goin', huh? We
probably all got things to do here.

65
00:06:38.723 --> 00:06:40.496
I figured we'd start out
with a five-minute break.

66
00:06:40.522 --> 00:06:41.851
There's one gentleman
in the bathroom.

67
00:06:41.852 --> 00:06:42.893
- Mr. Foreman.
- Huh?

68
00:06:42.894 --> 00:06:45.771
- Are, uh... Are we gonna sit in order?
- Gee, I don't know.

69
00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:47.623
I, uh... Well, I guess so.

70
00:06:52.529 --> 00:06:54.071
Uh, you're in my seat.

71
00:06:54.072 --> 00:06:56.173
- Oh. Excuse me.
- That's all right.

72
00:06:58.618 --> 00:07:00.469
Hey, that's not a bad view, huh?

73
00:07:03.999 --> 00:07:06.542
What did you think of the case?

74
00:07:06.543 --> 00:07:08.669
I mean, it had a lot
of interest for me.

75
00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.897
No real... dead spots.
You know what I mean?

76
00:07:12.674 --> 00:07:15.217
I tell you, we were lucky
to get a murder case.

77
00:07:15.218 --> 00:07:17.261
I figured us for an
assault or burglary.

78
00:07:17.262 --> 00:07:19.597
Boy, they can be the dullest.

79
00:07:21.099 --> 00:07:22.474
Hey.

80
00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:24.143
That the Woolworth Building?

81
00:07:24.144 --> 00:07:26.145
That's right.

82
00:07:26.146 --> 00:07:27.813
Isn't that funny?

83
00:07:27.814 --> 00:07:30.274
You know, I've lived here all my life.
I've never been inside that.

84
00:07:30.275 --> 00:07:32.443
If you had to
sort out all that junk...

85
00:07:32.444 --> 00:07:34.612
Like that thing with the movies.

86
00:07:34.613 --> 00:07:37.698
Yeah. You can say that again. And what
about that business with the knife?

87
00:07:37.699 --> 00:07:40.659
I mean, asking grown-up people
to believe that kind of jazz, huh?

88
00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:43.204
Well, I expected that.
You know what we're dealing with.

89
00:07:43.205 --> 00:07:45.748
Yeah, I guess so.

90
00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:47.541
Well, your horn works.
Now try your lights.

91
00:07:49.127 --> 00:07:50.669
- What do you got, a cold?
- And how.

92
00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.963
These hot-weather
colds can kill you.

93
00:07:52.964 --> 00:07:55.466
I can hardly touch my nose.
You know what I mean?

94
00:07:55.467 --> 00:07:59.094
I sure do. I just got over one.

95
00:07:59.095 --> 00:08:01.368
Aw, come on. Mr. Foreman,
let's go here, huh?

96
00:08:01.394 --> 00:08:03.641
Well, that guy's still
in the bathroom.

97
00:08:03.642 --> 00:08:06.560
Hey, what's new? I didn't get a
chance to see a paper this morning.

98
00:08:06.561 --> 00:08:07.728
Hmm?

99
00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:10.231
I was only wondering
how the market closed.

100
00:08:10.232 --> 00:08:13.025
You got a seat on the Exchange?

101
00:08:13.026 --> 00:08:14.860
I'm a broker.

102
00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:19.323
I run a messenger service...
the Beck and Call Company.

103
00:08:19.324 --> 00:08:21.533
The name is my wife's idea.

104
00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:25.246
Got 37 men working.
Started with nothing.

105
00:08:25.247 --> 00:08:28.499
Okay, men, let's
take our seats, huh?

106
00:08:31.586 --> 00:08:33.504
Yeah, we can all get outta
here pretty quick, huh?

107
00:08:33.505 --> 00:08:34.594
I don't know about the rest of you,

108
00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.799
but I happen to have tickets
to that ball game tonight.

109
00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.717
Yanks and Cleveland.

110
00:08:38.718 --> 00:08:40.594
Yeah, we got this kid,
Modjelewski, in there.

111
00:08:40.595 --> 00:08:42.346
Ooh, he's a real bull, this kid.

112
00:08:42.347 --> 00:08:44.556
You know... Phoom!

113
00:08:44.557 --> 00:08:46.892
A real jug handle. You know?

114
00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.228
Phoom!

115
00:08:49.229 --> 00:08:51.647
You're a real baseball fan,
aren't you? Huh? Yeah.

116
00:08:51.648 --> 00:08:53.190
Where do we sit here?

117
00:08:53.191 --> 00:08:56.360
Well, I thought we'd sit in order,
you know, by jury numbers.

118
00:08:56.361 --> 00:08:58.862
One, two, three, four, five...
so on, around the table.

119
00:08:58.863 --> 00:09:00.197
If that's okay with you gentlemen.

120
00:09:00.198 --> 00:09:01.365
- Sure.
- What's the difference?

121
00:09:01.366 --> 00:09:04.493
- I think it's reasonable to sit in order.
- Let it be.

122
00:09:04.494 --> 00:09:06.829
- Number 12. Do I sit down here?
- Yeah. That's 12. We go around...

123
00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:10.165
- We start with you. One, right?
- One, yeah. Two, three, four, five, six.

124
00:09:10.166 --> 00:09:12.584
What was your impression of
the prosecuting attorney?

125
00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:14.378
Okay. Two. You're two.

126
00:09:14.379 --> 00:09:15.629
I beg pardon?

127
00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:19.883
I thought he was really sharp, the way
he handled all those points one by one.

128
00:09:19.884 --> 00:09:23.095
Logical sequence.
I was very impressed.

129
00:09:23.096 --> 00:09:26.181
I think he... he did an expert job.

130
00:09:26.182 --> 00:09:29.268
A lot of drive too,
you know? Real drive.

131
00:09:29.269 --> 00:09:31.003
Okay, fellas, can we
hold it down a minute?

132
00:09:31.029 --> 00:09:31.437
Sure.

133
00:09:31.438 --> 00:09:33.105
Uh, fellas.

134
00:09:33.106 --> 00:09:36.667
Say, we'd like to get started.
Gentleman at the window.

135
00:09:38.236 --> 00:09:40.487
- We'd like to get started.
- Oh, I'm sorry.

136
00:09:40.488 --> 00:09:44.241
Pretty tough to figure, isn't it? Kid
kills his father... bing, just like that.

137
00:09:44.242 --> 00:09:46.910
Aw, listen, you analyze the figures,
you'll see it happens all the time.

138
00:09:46.911 --> 00:09:48.912
They let those kids
run wild up there.

139
00:09:48.913 --> 00:09:51.707
Well, maybe it serves 'em right.
You know what I mean?

140
00:09:51.708 --> 00:09:53.375
Is... Is everyone here?

141
00:09:53.376 --> 00:09:55.294
The old man is inside.

142
00:09:55.295 --> 00:09:58.005
Oh. Would you, uh,
knock on the door for him?

143
00:09:58.006 --> 00:10:00.049
You a Yankee fan?

144
00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:01.692
No. Baltimore.

145
00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.111
- Baltimore?
- Yeah.

146
00:10:05.305 --> 00:10:07.931
That's like being hit in the head
with a crowbar once a day.

147
00:10:09.601 --> 00:10:10.934
Who have they got?

148
00:10:10.935 --> 00:10:13.437
No, I mean, who have they got
besides good groundskeepers?

149
00:10:13.438 --> 00:10:15.064
Say, uh, we'd like to get started.

150
00:10:15.065 --> 00:10:18.208
Oh, forgive me, gentlemen.
I... I didn't mean to keep you waiting.

151
00:10:18.902 --> 00:10:20.778
Baltimore?

152
00:10:20.779 --> 00:10:23.655
Okay, gentlemen,
if I can have your attention.

153
00:10:23.656 --> 00:10:26.700
You fellas can handle this thing
any way that you want to.

154
00:10:26.701 --> 00:10:29.286
I'm, um... You know,
I'm not gonna make any rules.

155
00:10:29.287 --> 00:10:32.664
Uh, we can, well, discuss it
first and, uh, then vote on it.

156
00:10:32.665 --> 00:10:35.417
That's, of course, uh...
That's, uh, one way.

157
00:10:35.418 --> 00:10:38.462
And, uh, well, we can vote
on it right now, and...

158
00:10:38.463 --> 00:10:41.048
I think it's customary to
take a preliminary vote.

159
00:10:41.049 --> 00:10:44.718
Yeah, let's... Let's vote. Who knows?
Maybe we all can get outta here, huh?

160
00:10:44.719 --> 00:10:46.512
Uh-huh.

161
00:10:46.513 --> 00:10:49.681
Okay, then I think that, uh...

162
00:10:49.682 --> 00:10:52.643
Of course you know that we have a
first-degree murder charge here...

163
00:10:52.644 --> 00:10:54.812
and if we vote the
accused guilty...

164
00:10:54.813 --> 00:10:58.148
uh, we've got to send
him to the chair.

165
00:10:58.149 --> 00:11:01.985
- Um, that's mandatory.
- I think we know that.

166
00:11:01.986 --> 00:11:03.821
- Let's see who's where.
- We might as well.

167
00:11:03.822 --> 00:11:07.716
- Okay. Uh, anyone doesn't want to vote?
- All right with me.

168
00:11:08.827 --> 00:11:13.497
Okay. Then, uh, just remember that this
has to be 12 to nothing either way.

169
00:11:13.498 --> 00:11:15.040
Um, that's the law.

170
00:11:15.041 --> 00:11:17.167
Okay, are we ready?

171
00:11:17.168 --> 00:11:20.270
Now, all those voting guilty,
please raise your hands.

172
00:11:24.175 --> 00:11:27.778
One, two, three, four,
five, six, seven...

173
00:11:29.013 --> 00:11:31.682
eight, nine, 10, 11.

174
00:11:31.683 --> 00:11:34.685
Okay. That's 11 guilty.

175
00:11:34.686 --> 00:11:36.286
Those voting not guilty.

176
00:11:37.355 --> 00:11:39.523
One. Right.

177
00:11:39.524 --> 00:11:41.917
That's 11 guilty. One not guilty.

178
00:11:42.735 --> 00:11:44.403
Well, now we know where we are.

179
00:11:44.404 --> 00:11:48.674
Boy, oh, boy. There's always one.

180
00:11:49.117 --> 00:11:51.493
So, what do we do now?

181
00:11:51.494 --> 00:11:53.412
Well, I guess we talk.

182
00:11:53.413 --> 00:11:54.830
Boy, oh, boy.

183
00:11:54.831 --> 00:11:56.765
You really think he's innocent?

184
00:12:00.128 --> 00:12:01.753
I don't know.

185
00:12:01.754 --> 00:12:04.715
I mean, you sat in court with the
rest of us. You heard what we did.

186
00:12:04.716 --> 00:12:06.925
The kid's a dangerous killer.
You could see it.

187
00:12:06.926 --> 00:12:08.635
He's 18 years old.

188
00:12:08.636 --> 00:12:10.804
Well, that's old enough. He...

189
00:12:10.805 --> 00:12:13.557
He stabbed his own father
four inches into the chest.

190
00:12:13.558 --> 00:12:15.893
They proved it a dozen
different ways in court.

191
00:12:15.894 --> 00:12:18.520
Would you like me to
list them for you?

192
00:12:18.521 --> 00:12:19.897
No.

193
00:12:19.898 --> 00:12:22.065
Then what do you want?

194
00:12:22.066 --> 00:12:25.068
- I just want to talk.
- What's there to talk about?

195
00:12:25.069 --> 00:12:26.487
Eleven men in here
think he's guilty.

196
00:12:26.488 --> 00:12:28.822
No one had to think about
it twice except you.

197
00:12:28.823 --> 00:12:32.117
I want to ask you something.
Do you believe his story?

198
00:12:32.118 --> 00:12:34.912
I don't know whether I believe
it or not. Maybe I don't.

199
00:12:34.913 --> 00:12:36.580
So how come you vote not guilty?

200
00:12:36.581 --> 00:12:38.582
Well, there were 11
votes for guilty.

201
00:12:38.583 --> 00:12:41.418
It's not easy to raise my hand
and send a boy off to die...

202
00:12:41.419 --> 00:12:43.587
without talking about it first.

203
00:12:43.588 --> 00:12:46.106
- Well, now, who says it's easy?
- No one.

204
00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.551
What, just because I voted fast?

205
00:12:49.552 --> 00:12:52.054
I honestly think the guy's guilty.

206
00:12:52.055 --> 00:12:54.932
You couldn't change my mind if
you talked for a hundred years.

207
00:12:54.933 --> 00:12:57.034
I'm not trying to change
your mind. It's just that...

208
00:12:57.519 --> 00:12:59.853
We're talking about
somebody's life here.

209
00:12:59.854 --> 00:13:02.022
We can't decide it in five minutes.
Supposing we're wrong.

210
00:13:02.023 --> 00:13:03.524
"Supposing we're wrong."

211
00:13:03.525 --> 00:13:05.609
Supposing this whole building
should fall down on my head.

212
00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.711
- You can suppose anything.
- That's right.

213
00:13:08.446 --> 00:13:10.280
Look, what's the difference
how long it takes?

214
00:13:10.281 --> 00:13:12.950
Suppose we do it in
five minutes. So what?

215
00:13:12.951 --> 00:13:15.160
Let's take an hour.

216
00:13:15.161 --> 00:13:17.179
The ball game doesn't
start till 8:00.

217
00:13:19.791 --> 00:13:21.058
Hmm?

218
00:13:23.127 --> 00:13:25.045
Who's got something to say?

219
00:13:25.046 --> 00:13:26.547
I'm willing to sit for an hour.

220
00:13:26.548 --> 00:13:29.341
Great. I heard a pretty
good story last night.

221
00:13:29.342 --> 00:13:31.526
That's not why we're sitting here.

222
00:13:32.136 --> 00:13:35.781
All right, then you tell me.
What are we sitting here for?

223
00:13:36.933 --> 00:13:38.867
I don't know. Maybe no reason.

224
00:13:39.769 --> 00:13:43.313
Look, this kid's been kicked
around all of his life...

225
00:13:43.314 --> 00:13:47.317
you know, born in a slum,
mother dead since he was nine.

226
00:13:47.318 --> 00:13:49.611
He lived for a year and a half
in an orphanage when his...

227
00:13:49.612 --> 00:13:52.614
his father was serving a
jail term for forgery.

228
00:13:52.615 --> 00:13:55.033
It's not a very happy beginning.

229
00:13:55.034 --> 00:13:57.244
He's a... a wild, angry kid.

230
00:13:57.245 --> 00:13:59.037
That's all he's ever been.
And you know why?

231
00:13:59.038 --> 00:14:02.833
'Cause he's been hit on the head
by somebody once a day every day.

232
00:14:02.834 --> 00:14:05.127
He's had a... He's had a
pretty miserable 18 years.

233
00:14:05.128 --> 00:14:08.171
I... I just think we owe
him a few words. That's all.

234
00:14:08.172 --> 00:14:11.800
I don't mind telling you this, mister.
We don't owe him a thing.

235
00:14:11.801 --> 00:14:14.970
He got a fair trial, didn't he?
What do you think that trial cost?

236
00:14:14.971 --> 00:14:16.935
He's lucky he got it.
You know what I mean?

237
00:14:16.961 --> 00:14:20.559
- You know it.
- Now, look, we're all grown-ups in here.

238
00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.519
We heard the facts, didn't we?

239
00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:26.398
You're not gonna tell me that we're supposed
to believe this kid, knowing what he is.

240
00:14:26.399 --> 00:14:28.859
Listen, I've lived among
them all my life.

241
00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.279
You can't believe a word they say.
You know that.

242
00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.197
I mean, they're born liars.

243
00:14:34.198 --> 00:14:37.618
- Only an ignorant man can believe that.
- Now listen...

244
00:14:37.619 --> 00:14:40.537
Do you think you were born
with a monopoly on the truth?

245
00:14:40.538 --> 00:14:43.206
I think certain things should
be pointed out to this man.

246
00:14:43.207 --> 00:14:45.375
Come on. This isn't Sunday.
We don't need a sermon.

247
00:14:45.376 --> 00:14:47.728
Come on. We have a job
to do. Now let's do it.

248
00:14:48.671 --> 00:14:51.815
Rice Pops. It's a product
I work on at the agency.

249
00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:55.260
"The breakfast with
the built-in bounce."

250
00:14:55.261 --> 00:14:56.970
- I wrote that line.
- Very catchy.

251
00:14:56.971 --> 00:14:59.222
- Yeah.
- Say, do you mind?

252
00:14:59.223 --> 00:15:01.016
Oh, I'm sorry.

253
00:15:01.017 --> 00:15:03.560
I have this habit of doodling.
Keeps me thinking clearly.

254
00:15:03.561 --> 00:15:05.354
Yeah, we have all this work to do.

255
00:15:05.355 --> 00:15:07.147
- There's no point staying here forever.
- Sorry.

256
00:15:07.148 --> 00:15:07.981
Okay. Um...

257
00:15:07.982 --> 00:15:09.441
Okay. Um...

258
00:15:09.442 --> 00:15:13.320
perhaps if the gentleman down there
who's disagreeing with us...

259
00:15:13.321 --> 00:15:16.698
Well, perhaps you could tell us why. You
know, let us know what you're thinking.

260
00:15:16.699 --> 00:15:19.159
And we might be able to show
you where you're mixed up.

261
00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.911
Well, look, maybe...
Maybe this is an idea.

262
00:15:20.912 --> 00:15:23.413
Now, I haven't given it much
thought, but it seems to me...

263
00:15:23.414 --> 00:15:25.768
that it's up to the
group of us to convince

264
00:15:25.794 --> 00:15:27.918
this gentleman that he's
wrong and we're right.

265
00:15:27.919 --> 00:15:30.562
Now, maybe if we each took a
couple of minutes just to...

266
00:15:31.881 --> 00:15:33.382
Well, it was just a quick idea.

267
00:15:33.383 --> 00:15:34.925
No, no, no. That's a good one.

268
00:15:34.926 --> 00:15:38.095
Uh, suppose we go once
around the table.

269
00:15:38.096 --> 00:15:41.056
- I guess you're first.
- Oh.

270
00:15:41.057 --> 00:15:42.491
Well, uh...

271
00:15:43.935 --> 00:15:46.453
Well, it-it's hard
to put into words.

272
00:15:47.397 --> 00:15:49.481
I just think he's guilty.

273
00:15:49.482 --> 00:15:52.067
I thought it was obvious
from the word "go."

274
00:15:52.068 --> 00:15:53.652
I mean, nobody proved otherwise.

275
00:15:53.653 --> 00:15:55.278
Nobody has to prove otherwise.

276
00:15:55.279 --> 00:15:57.614
The burden of proof's
on the prosecution.

277
00:15:57.615 --> 00:16:00.158
The defendant doesn't even
have to open his mouth.

278
00:16:00.159 --> 00:16:03.370
- That's in the Constitution.
- Oh, well, sure, I know that.

279
00:16:03.371 --> 00:16:05.430
Uh, what I meant was, is...

280
00:16:07.125 --> 00:16:09.209
Well, I just think he's guilty.

281
00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:11.002
I-I mean, somebody saw him do it.

282
00:16:11.003 --> 00:16:16.133
Okay. Uh, here's what I think, and I
have no personal feelings about this.

283
00:16:16.134 --> 00:16:18.401
I just want to talk about facts.

284
00:16:19.303 --> 00:16:21.530
Number one.

285
00:16:21.973 --> 00:16:26.184
The old man lived downstairs under the
room where the killing took place.

286
00:16:26.185 --> 00:16:28.395
At 10 minutes after 12:00
on the night of the killing...

287
00:16:28.396 --> 00:16:32.149
he heard loud noises,
said it sounded like a fight.

288
00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:35.110
And he heard the kid yell out,
"I'm gonna kill ya."

289
00:16:35.111 --> 00:16:37.487
A second later, he heard
a body hit the floor.

290
00:16:37.488 --> 00:16:39.281
Ran to the door, opened it up...

291
00:16:39.282 --> 00:16:41.783
saw the kid running down the
stairs and out of the house.

292
00:16:41.784 --> 00:16:43.160
Called the police.

293
00:16:43.161 --> 00:16:45.912
They came in, found the old man
with a knife in his chest.

294
00:16:45.913 --> 00:16:49.249
The coroner fixed the time
of death around midnight.

295
00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:52.419
Now these are facts.
You can't refute facts.

296
00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.671
The kid is guilty.

297
00:16:54.672 --> 00:16:56.757
I'm just as sentimental
as the next fella.

298
00:16:56.758 --> 00:17:00.719
I know he's only 18, but he's still
gotta pay for what he did.

299
00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.262
I'm with you.

300
00:17:02.263 --> 00:17:04.890
Okay. Are you finished?

301
00:17:04.891 --> 00:17:06.433
- Yeah.
- Right. Next.

302
00:17:06.434 --> 00:17:09.728
It is obvious, to me anyway,
that the boy's entire story was flimsy.

303
00:17:09.729 --> 00:17:12.022
He claimed he was at the movies
during the time of the killing...

304
00:17:12.023 --> 00:17:14.649
and yet, one hour later, he couldn't
remember the names of the films he saw...

305
00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:16.568
- or who played in them.
- That's right.

306
00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:18.945
And no one saw him going
in or out of the theater.

307
00:17:18.946 --> 00:17:20.655
Listen, what about the
woman across the street?

308
00:17:20.656 --> 00:17:23.033
If her testimony don't
prove it, nothing does.

309
00:17:23.034 --> 00:17:25.452
That's right. She was the one
who actually saw the killing.

310
00:17:25.453 --> 00:17:27.954
Now, fellas, please.
Let's go in order here, huh?

311
00:17:27.955 --> 00:17:31.291
Just a minute. Here's a woman...

312
00:17:31.292 --> 00:17:33.835
Here's a woman who was
lying in bed. She can't sleep.

313
00:17:33.836 --> 00:17:35.837
She's dying with the heat.
You know what I mean?

314
00:17:35.838 --> 00:17:37.756
Anyway, she looks out the window...

315
00:17:37.757 --> 00:17:41.426
and right across the street she sees
the kid stick the knife into his father.

316
00:17:41.427 --> 00:17:44.387
The time is 12:10 on the nose.

317
00:17:44.388 --> 00:17:48.266
Everything fits. Look, she's
known the kid all his life.

318
00:17:48.267 --> 00:17:50.477
His window is right opposite
hers across the el tracks...

319
00:17:50.478 --> 00:17:52.229
and she swore she saw him do it.

320
00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.431
Through the windows of
the passing el train.

321
00:17:54.457 --> 00:17:59.236
Right. This el train had no passengers
on it. It was just being moved downtown.

322
00:17:59.237 --> 00:18:02.239
The lights were out, remember?
And they proved in court that, at night...

323
00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:04.741
you can look through the windows of an
el train when the lights are out...

324
00:18:04.742 --> 00:18:07.160
and see what's happening
on the other side.

325
00:18:07.161 --> 00:18:10.080
- They proved it.
- I'd like to ask you something.

326
00:18:10.081 --> 00:18:12.833
You don't believe the boy's story.
How come you believe the woman's?

327
00:18:12.834 --> 00:18:14.684
She's one of them too, isn't she?

328
00:18:17.255 --> 00:18:19.339
You're a pretty smart
fella, aren't you?

329
00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:20.841
- Okay, gentlemen.
- Now, now.

330
00:18:20.842 --> 00:18:23.134
- Now, gentlemen.
- Come on. Sit down. Sit down.

331
00:18:23.135 --> 00:18:25.762
Oh, what's he so wise about?
I'm telling you.

332
00:18:25.763 --> 00:18:29.099
Okay, now come on. Now we're
not gonna get anywhere fighting.

333
00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:30.433
Whose turn is it next?

334
00:18:30.434 --> 00:18:33.370
Oh, uh, his. Number five.

335
00:18:35.523 --> 00:18:38.375
Okay, may I... Can I pass? it...

336
00:18:39.068 --> 00:18:41.194
Well, uh, that's your privilege.

337
00:18:41.195 --> 00:18:44.406
Uh, how about the next gentleman?

338
00:18:44.407 --> 00:18:47.158
Oh. Well, uh, I don't know.

339
00:18:47.159 --> 00:18:51.471
I started to be convinced,
you know, very early in the case.

340
00:18:51.956 --> 00:18:56.167
You see, I was looking for a motive.
That's very important.

341
00:18:56.168 --> 00:19:00.463
Because if you don't have a motive,
where's your case, right?

342
00:19:00.464 --> 00:19:03.633
Anyway, that, uh, testimony
from those people...

343
00:19:03.634 --> 00:19:06.761
in the apartment across the hall
from the kid's apartment...

344
00:19:06.762 --> 00:19:09.097
that was very powerful.

345
00:19:09.098 --> 00:19:11.975
Didn't they say something about a...
a fight and an argument...

346
00:19:11.976 --> 00:19:16.479
between the old man and his son
around about, uh, 7:00 that night?

347
00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:19.024
- I mean, I could be wrong, but I...
- It was 8:00.

348
00:19:19.025 --> 00:19:21.151
It was 8:00. That's right.
They heard an argument.

349
00:19:21.152 --> 00:19:22.986
They couldn't hear
what it was about.

350
00:19:22.987 --> 00:19:25.030
Then they heard the
father hit the boy twice.

351
00:19:25.031 --> 00:19:27.144
Finally they saw the boy run...

352
00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:29.743
angrily out of the house.
What does that prove?

353
00:19:29.744 --> 00:19:33.496
Well, it don't exactly prove anything.
It's just part of the picture.

354
00:19:33.497 --> 00:19:36.499
Well, you said it provided a motive. The
prosecuting attorney said the same thing.

355
00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.835
I don't think that was
a very strong motive.

356
00:19:38.836 --> 00:19:42.505
This boy's been hit so many times in his
life that violence is practically a...

357
00:19:42.506 --> 00:19:44.966
It's a normal state
of affairs with him.

358
00:19:44.967 --> 00:19:49.262
I just... I can't see two slaps in the face
provoking him into committing murder.

359
00:19:49.263 --> 00:19:52.949
It may have been two too many.
Everyone has a breaking point.

360
00:19:55.394 --> 00:19:57.562
Anything else?

361
00:19:57.563 --> 00:20:00.106
- No.
- Okay.

362
00:20:00.107 --> 00:20:01.958
Uh, how about you?

363
00:20:03.694 --> 00:20:05.754
I don't know. It's all been said.

364
00:20:07.198 --> 00:20:09.924
You can talk here forever.
It's still the same thing.

365
00:20:11.035 --> 00:20:14.079
This kid is five for 0.

366
00:20:14.080 --> 00:20:16.039
Well, look at his record.

367
00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:18.333
When he was 10,
he was in children's court.

368
00:20:18.334 --> 00:20:20.210
He threw a rock at a teacher.

369
00:20:20.211 --> 00:20:22.587
When he was 15,
he was in reform school.

370
00:20:22.588 --> 00:20:24.506
He stole a car.

371
00:20:24.507 --> 00:20:27.092
He's been arrested for mugging.

372
00:20:27.093 --> 00:20:29.177
He was picked up twice
for knife fighting.

373
00:20:29.178 --> 00:20:31.346
Oh, yeah, they say he's
real handy with a knife.

374
00:20:31.347 --> 00:20:34.224
Oh, this is a very fine boy.

375
00:20:34.225 --> 00:20:37.310
Ever since he was five years old,
his father beat him up regularly.

376
00:20:37.311 --> 00:20:39.813
- He used his fists.
- Well, so would I.

377
00:20:39.814 --> 00:20:41.398
A kid like that?

378
00:20:41.399 --> 00:20:44.667
It's these kids, the way
they are nowadays.

379
00:20:47.071 --> 00:20:50.340
When I was a kid,
I used to call my father "sir."

380
00:20:50.992 --> 00:20:52.367
That's right. "Sir."

381
00:20:52.368 --> 00:20:54.411
Do you ever hear a kid call
his father that any more?

382
00:20:54.412 --> 00:20:57.013
Fathers don't seem to think
it's important any more.

383
00:20:57.707 --> 00:20:59.849
- You got any kids?
- Three.

384
00:21:01.127 --> 00:21:02.560
I got one.

385
00:21:03.963 --> 00:21:05.814
Twenty-two years old.

386
00:21:11.846 --> 00:21:14.697
When he was nine years old,
he ran away from a fight.

387
00:21:16.308 --> 00:21:19.577
I saw it. I was so embarrassed
I almost threw up.

388
00:21:21.147 --> 00:21:25.875
I said, "I'm gonna make a man out of you
if I have to break you in two trying."

389
00:21:28.279 --> 00:21:30.296
Well, I made a man out of him.

390
00:21:31.198 --> 00:21:33.466
When he was 16, we had a fight.

391
00:21:34.952 --> 00:21:37.428
Hit me in the jaw.
He was a big kid.

392
00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:42.934
Haven't seen him for two years.

393
00:21:44.754 --> 00:21:47.147
Kids.

394
00:21:48.758 --> 00:21:50.817
Work your heart out.

395
00:21:53.304 --> 00:21:54.637
Well, let's get going.

396
00:21:54.638 --> 00:21:57.057
I think we're missing
the point here.

397
00:21:57.058 --> 00:22:00.560
This boy, let's say he's the product of a
broken home and a filthy neighborhood.

398
00:22:00.561 --> 00:22:04.230
We can't help that. We're here to decide
whether he's innocent or guilty...

399
00:22:04.231 --> 00:22:07.233
not to go into the reasons why
he grew up the way he did.

400
00:22:07.234 --> 00:22:10.028
He was born in a slum. Slums are
breeding grounds for criminals.

401
00:22:10.029 --> 00:22:11.988
I know it, and so do you.

402
00:22:11.989 --> 00:22:14.247
It's no secret children
from slum backgrounds

403
00:22:14.273 --> 00:22:16.485
are potential menaces to society.

404
00:22:16.786 --> 00:22:19.037
- Now I think...
- Brother, you can say that again.

405
00:22:19.038 --> 00:22:21.308
The kids who crawl out of
these places are real trash.

406
00:22:21.334 --> 00:22:24.538
- I don't want any part of them.
I'm telling you.
- Listen, mister. Listen.

407
00:22:24.564 --> 00:22:26.633
I, uh... I've lived in
a slum all my life.

408
00:22:26.659 --> 00:22:28.497
- Wait a minute.
- Please, I...

409
00:22:28.523 --> 00:22:31.299
I played in backyards that
were filled with garbage.

410
00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.426
I mean, maybe you can
still smell it on me.

411
00:22:33.427 --> 00:22:34.844
Now, listen, sonny.

412
00:22:34.845 --> 00:22:36.513
Come on now. There's nothing
personal about this.

413
00:22:36.514 --> 00:22:39.140
- No, there was something personal.
- Aw, come on, fella. He didn't mean you.

414
00:22:39.141 --> 00:22:41.309
Let's not be so sensitive.

415
00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:43.228
This sensitivity I can understand.

416
00:22:43.229 --> 00:22:47.957
Okay, look, let's stop the arguing.
We're only wasting time, and...

417
00:22:49.026 --> 00:22:51.252
Look, your turn down
there. Let's go.

418
00:22:52.488 --> 00:22:55.406
Oh, I didn't expect a turn. I thought
you were all gonna try to convince me.

419
00:22:55.407 --> 00:22:57.242
- Wasn't that the idea?
- Check. That was the idea.

420
00:22:57.243 --> 00:22:59.536
- Oh, I forgot about that. He's right.
- What difference does it make?

421
00:22:59.537 --> 00:23:01.955
He's the one who's keeping us in here.
Let's hear what he's got to say.

422
00:23:01.956 --> 00:23:04.749
Oh, now wait a minute. We decided
to do this a certain way.

423
00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:06.459
I think we ought to
stick to that way.

424
00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:08.545
Oh, stop being a kid, will you?

425
00:23:08.546 --> 00:23:10.630
- What do you mean "a kid"?
- What do you think I mean?

426
00:23:10.631 --> 00:23:12.357
K-I-D. Kid.

427
00:23:14.844 --> 00:23:18.596
What? Listen, just because I'm
trying to keep this thing organized?

428
00:23:18.597 --> 00:23:20.807
Here, you take it. You take
on the responsibility.

429
00:23:20.808 --> 00:23:22.767
I'll just... I'll keep my
mouth shut. That's all.

430
00:23:22.768 --> 00:23:24.853
What are you getting so hot about?
Calm down, will you?

431
00:23:24.854 --> 00:23:26.271
Don't tell me to calm down.

432
00:23:26.272 --> 00:23:28.481
Here. You wanna take the chair,
just take the chair. That's all.

433
00:23:28.482 --> 00:23:31.025
- Did you ever see such a thing?
- See if you can keep it running.

434
00:23:31.026 --> 00:23:32.281
Listen, you think it's
funny or something?

435
00:23:32.307 --> 00:23:34.737
Hey, forget it, fella. The whole
thing's unimportant. Come on.

436
00:23:34.738 --> 00:23:37.949
- Unimportant? Oh, here, you try it.
- No, nobody wants to change.

437
00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.784
You're doing a beautiful job.
Sit down.

438
00:23:39.785 --> 00:23:42.704
Yeah, you're doing great.
Just great, fella.

439
00:23:42.705 --> 00:23:44.681
You stay in there and
pitch. You know?

440
00:23:45.541 --> 00:23:47.850
All right. Let's hear
from somebody.

441
00:23:49.253 --> 00:23:53.423
Well, if you want me to tell you how
I feel about it, it's all right with me.

442
00:23:53.424 --> 00:23:55.633
Boy, I don't care what you do.

443
00:23:55.634 --> 00:23:58.595
All right, I don't have
anything brilliant.

444
00:23:58.596 --> 00:24:00.805
I only know as much as you do.

445
00:24:00.806 --> 00:24:04.642
According to the testimony,
the boy looks guilty. Maybe he is.

446
00:24:04.643 --> 00:24:08.913
I sat there in court for six days
listening while the evidence built up.

447
00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:11.232
Everybody sounded so
positive, you know?

448
00:24:11.233 --> 00:24:13.776
I-I began to get a peculiar
feeling about this trial.

449
00:24:13.777 --> 00:24:15.945
I mean, nothing is that positive.

450
00:24:15.946 --> 00:24:18.156
There are a lot of questions
I'd have liked to ask.

451
00:24:18.157 --> 00:24:20.950
I don't know. Maybe they wouldn't
have meant anything, but...

452
00:24:20.951 --> 00:24:22.994
I began to get the feeling
that the defense counsel...

453
00:24:22.995 --> 00:24:25.580
wasn't conducting a thorough-enough
cross-examination.

454
00:24:25.581 --> 00:24:29.042
I mean, he... he let too many
things go by, little things that...

455
00:24:29.043 --> 00:24:31.878
What little things? Listen, when
these fellas don't ask questions...

456
00:24:31.879 --> 00:24:34.464
it's because they know the answers
already and they figure they'll be hurt.

457
00:24:34.465 --> 00:24:38.468
Maybe it's also possible for a lawyer
to be just plain stupid, isn't it?

458
00:24:38.469 --> 00:24:39.844
I mean it's possible.

459
00:24:39.845 --> 00:24:41.646
You sound like you met
my brother-in-law once.

460
00:24:44.600 --> 00:24:47.410
I... I kept putting myself
in the kid's place.

461
00:24:49.146 --> 00:24:51.356
I'd have asked for
another lawyer, I think.

462
00:24:51.357 --> 00:24:53.983
I mean, if I was on
trial for my life...

463
00:24:53.984 --> 00:24:58.863
I'd want my lawyer to tear the prosecution
witnesses to shreds, or at least try to.

464
00:24:58.864 --> 00:25:02.925
Look, there was one alleged
eyewitness to this killing.

465
00:25:03.369 --> 00:25:06.829
Someone else claims he heard the killing,
saw the boy run out afterwards.

466
00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:09.040
And there was a lot of
circumstantial evidence.

467
00:25:09.041 --> 00:25:12.627
But actually, those two witnesses were
the entire case for the prosecution.

468
00:25:12.628 --> 00:25:14.504
Supposing they're wrong.

469
00:25:14.505 --> 00:25:16.381
What do you mean
supposing they're wrong?

470
00:25:16.382 --> 00:25:18.341
What's the point of
having witnesses at all?

471
00:25:18.342 --> 00:25:21.052
- Could they be wrong?
- What are you trying to say?

472
00:25:21.053 --> 00:25:22.679
Those people sat on
the stand under oath.

473
00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:24.305
They're only people.
People make mistakes.

474
00:25:24.306 --> 00:25:26.115
Could they be wrong?

475
00:25:26.809 --> 00:25:29.811
- Well, no, I don't think so.
- Do you know so?

476
00:25:29.812 --> 00:25:31.729
Oh, come on. Nobody can
know a thing like that.

477
00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:33.523
This isn't an exact science.

478
00:25:33.524 --> 00:25:34.957
That's right. It isn't.

479
00:25:40.030 --> 00:25:42.865
Okay, let...
Let's get to the point.

480
00:25:42.866 --> 00:25:45.535
What about the switch knife they
found in the old man's chest?

481
00:25:45.536 --> 00:25:48.037
Uh, wait-wait a minute. There's
some people who haven't talked yet.

482
00:25:48.038 --> 00:25:50.123
- Shouldn't we go in order?
- They'll get a chance to talk.

483
00:25:50.124 --> 00:25:52.542
Be quiet a second, will you?

484
00:25:52.543 --> 00:25:53.918
What about it, this...

485
00:25:53.919 --> 00:25:57.463
the knife this fine upright boy admitted
buying the night of the killing?

486
00:25:57.464 --> 00:25:58.881
Let's talk about it.

487
00:25:58.882 --> 00:26:01.217
All right, let's talk about it.
Let's get it in here and look at it.

488
00:26:01.218 --> 00:26:03.052
I'd like to see it again.
Mr. Foreman?

489
00:26:03.053 --> 00:26:06.222
We all saw what it looks like.
Why do we have to see it again?

490
00:26:06.223 --> 00:26:08.343
The gentleman has a right
to see exhibits in evidence.

491
00:26:10.394 --> 00:26:11.936
Say, could you bring us the knife?

492
00:26:11.937 --> 00:26:13.830
- Knife?
- Yeah. Thank you.

493
00:26:15.399 --> 00:26:18.735
The knife and the way it was bought is
pretty strong evidence, don't you think?

494
00:26:18.736 --> 00:26:20.486
- I do.
- Good.

495
00:26:20.487 --> 00:26:23.072
Now, suppose we take these
facts one at a time.

496
00:26:23.073 --> 00:26:26.409
One. The boy admitted going out of the
house at 8:00 on the night of the murder...

497
00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:28.411
after being slapped several
times by his father.

498
00:26:28.412 --> 00:26:31.247
No, no. No, he didn't say
"slapped." He said "punched."

499
00:26:31.248 --> 00:26:33.750
There's a difference between
a slap and a punch.

500
00:26:33.751 --> 00:26:36.419
After being hit several
times by his father.

501
00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:38.755
Two. He went directly to a
neighborhood junk shop...

502
00:26:38.756 --> 00:26:40.965
- where he bought one of those...
- Switch knives.

503
00:26:40.966 --> 00:26:43.051
Switchblade knives.

504
00:26:43.052 --> 00:26:44.927
This wasn't what you'd
call an ordinary knife.

505
00:26:44.928 --> 00:26:47.513
It had a very unusual
carved handle and blade.

506
00:26:47.514 --> 00:26:49.098
The storekeeper who
sold it to him...

507
00:26:49.099 --> 00:26:52.310
said it was the only one of its
kind he had ever had in stock.

508
00:26:52.311 --> 00:26:56.147
Three. He met some friends of his
in front of a tavern about 8:45.

509
00:26:56.148 --> 00:26:57.732
Am I right so far?

510
00:26:57.733 --> 00:26:59.942
- Yes, you are.
- You bet he is.

511
00:26:59.943 --> 00:27:03.196
He talked with his friends for about
an hour, leaving them at 9:45.

512
00:27:03.197 --> 00:27:06.240
During this time,
they saw the switch knife.

513
00:27:06.241 --> 00:27:08.117
Four.

514
00:27:08.118 --> 00:27:10.620
They identified the death
weapon in court...

515
00:27:10.621 --> 00:27:13.206
as that very same knife.

516
00:27:13.207 --> 00:27:16.084
Five. He arrived
home at about 10:00.

517
00:27:16.085 --> 00:27:18.503
Now this is where the stories
offered by the State and the boy...

518
00:27:18.504 --> 00:27:20.505
begin to diverge slightly.

519
00:27:20.506 --> 00:27:23.174
He claims that he went to
a movie at about 11:30...

520
00:27:23.175 --> 00:27:27.303
returning home at 3:10 to find his
father dead and himself arrested.

521
00:27:27.304 --> 00:27:29.430
He also claims that the two
detectives who arrested him...

522
00:27:29.431 --> 00:27:31.808
threw him down a half
a flight of stairs.

523
00:27:31.809 --> 00:27:33.726
Now, what happened
to the switch knife?

524
00:27:33.727 --> 00:27:36.437
He claims that it fell through a hole in
his pocket on the way to the movies...

525
00:27:36.438 --> 00:27:41.192
sometime between 11:30 and 3:10
and that he never saw it again.

526
00:27:41.193 --> 00:27:43.236
Now there's a tale, gentlemen.

527
00:27:43.237 --> 00:27:46.823
I think it's quite clear that the boy
never went to the movies that night.

528
00:27:46.824 --> 00:27:48.866
No one in the house saw
him go out at 11:30.

529
00:27:48.867 --> 00:27:50.451
No one at the theater
identified him.

530
00:27:50.452 --> 00:27:52.772
He couldn't even remember the
names of the pictures he saw.

531
00:27:55.624 --> 00:27:57.417
- What actually happened is this.
- Thank you.

532
00:27:57.418 --> 00:28:00.711
The boy stayed home, had another
fight with his father...

533
00:28:00.712 --> 00:28:04.507
stabbed him to death and left the
house at 10 minutes after 12:00.

534
00:28:04.508 --> 00:28:07.343
He even remembered to wipe the
knife clean of fingerprints.

535
00:28:07.344 --> 00:28:10.054
Now are you trying to tell
me that this knife...

536
00:28:10.055 --> 00:28:12.056
really fell through a hole
in the boy's pocket...

537
00:28:12.057 --> 00:28:14.517
someone picked it up off the street,
went to the boy's house...

538
00:28:14.518 --> 00:28:17.270
and stabbed his father with it
just to test its sharpness?

539
00:28:17.271 --> 00:28:19.355
No. I'm just saying it's possible
the boy lost his knife...

540
00:28:19.356 --> 00:28:21.649
and that somebody else stabbed
his father with a similar knife.

541
00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:22.942
It's just possible.

542
00:28:22.943 --> 00:28:24.627
Take a look at this knife.

543
00:28:26.447 --> 00:28:28.739
It's a very unusual knife.

544
00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:30.366
I've never seen one like it.

545
00:28:30.367 --> 00:28:33.244
Neither had the storekeeper
who sold it to the boy.

546
00:28:33.245 --> 00:28:36.289
Aren't you asking us to accept
a pretty incredible coincidence?

547
00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:38.708
I'm just saying a
coincidence is possible.

548
00:28:38.709 --> 00:28:40.309
And I say it's not possible.

549
00:28:46.842 --> 00:28:48.634
Where did that come from?

550
00:28:48.635 --> 00:28:50.094
- Where'd you get that?
- It's the same knife.

551
00:28:50.095 --> 00:28:51.679
What do you think you're doing?

552
00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:53.389
Where did you get it?

553
00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:55.725
I went out walking for a
couple of hours last night.

554
00:28:55.726 --> 00:28:57.810
I walked through the
boy's neighborhood.

555
00:28:57.811 --> 00:29:00.897
I bought that at a little pawnshop
just two blocks from the boy's house.

556
00:29:00.898 --> 00:29:02.440
It cost six dollars.

557
00:29:02.441 --> 00:29:05.651
It's against the law to buy
or sell switchblade knives.

558
00:29:05.652 --> 00:29:07.987
That's right. I broke the law.

559
00:29:07.988 --> 00:29:12.074
Listen, you pulled a real bright trick.
Now, supposing you tell me what it proves.

560
00:29:12.075 --> 00:29:14.243
Maybe there are 10
knives like that. So what?

561
00:29:14.244 --> 00:29:17.121
- Maybe there are.
- Well, what does it mean?

562
00:29:17.122 --> 00:29:20.500
You found another knife like it. What's that,
the discovery of the age or something?

563
00:29:20.501 --> 00:29:22.919
You mean you're asking us to believe
that somebody else did the stabbing...

564
00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:24.795
with exactly the
same kind of knife?

565
00:29:24.796 --> 00:29:26.130
The odds are a million-to-one.

566
00:29:26.131 --> 00:29:28.941
- It's possible.
- But not very probable.

567
00:29:30.052 --> 00:29:32.136
Okay, fellas, let's take our seats.

568
00:29:32.137 --> 00:29:34.298
There's no point in standing
around all over the place.

569
00:29:35.807 --> 00:29:39.310
You know, it's interesting that he'd find a
knife exactly like the one the boy bought.

570
00:29:39.311 --> 00:29:42.146
What's interesting about it?
"Interesting."

571
00:29:42.147 --> 00:29:44.106
Well, I don't know. I just
thought it was interesting.

572
00:29:44.107 --> 00:29:45.983
There are still 11 of us
here who think he's guilty.

573
00:29:45.984 --> 00:29:48.361
Right. What do you think
you're gonna accomplish?

574
00:29:48.362 --> 00:29:50.279
You're not gonna
change anybody's mind.

575
00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:52.740
So if you wanna be stubborn
and hang this jury, go ahead.

576
00:29:52.741 --> 00:29:55.885
The kid will be tried again and
found guilty sure as he's born.

577
00:29:57.329 --> 00:29:59.288
That's probably right.

578
00:29:59.289 --> 00:30:02.583
So what are you gonna do?
You know, we could be here all night.

579
00:30:02.584 --> 00:30:05.920
It's only one night.
A boy may die.

580
00:30:05.921 --> 00:30:08.548
Well, why don't we just
set up house here, huh?

581
00:30:08.549 --> 00:30:11.092
Someone send for Potsy the
policeman and get a pinochle deck...

582
00:30:11.093 --> 00:30:13.678
and, uh, we'll just sweat the
whole thing out right here.

583
00:30:15.097 --> 00:30:18.224
- I don't think he ought to joke about it.
- What do you want me to do about it?

584
00:30:18.225 --> 00:30:21.978
Oh, listen! I don't see what all this stuff
about the knife's got to do with anything.

585
00:30:21.979 --> 00:30:24.981
Somebody saw the kid stab his father.
What more do we need?

586
00:30:24.982 --> 00:30:27.858
You guys can talk the ears right
off my head. You know what I mean?

587
00:30:27.859 --> 00:30:30.653
I got three garages of mine goin'
to pot while you're talking.

588
00:30:30.654 --> 00:30:32.405
So let's get done
and get outta here.

589
00:30:32.406 --> 00:30:35.908
The knife was very important to the
district attorney. He spent a whole day...

590
00:30:35.909 --> 00:30:38.452
He's a 15th assistant or something.
What does he know about it?

591
00:30:38.453 --> 00:30:39.996
Hey, let's hold it down.

592
00:30:39.997 --> 00:30:42.431
These side arguments
are only slowing us up.

593
00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:46.669
Well, what about it?

594
00:30:46.670 --> 00:30:48.270
You're the only one.

595
00:30:56.096 --> 00:30:58.239
I have a proposition
to make to all of you.

596
00:30:59.224 --> 00:31:01.075
I'm gonna call for another vote.

597
00:31:02.436 --> 00:31:07.732
I want you 11 men to vote
by secret written ballot.

598
00:31:07.733 --> 00:31:09.859
I'll abstain.

599
00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:12.737
If there are 11 votes for guilty,
I won't stand alone.

600
00:31:12.738 --> 00:31:16.632
We'll take in a guilty verdict
to the judge right now.

601
00:31:17.993 --> 00:31:21.804
But if anyone votes not guilty,
we stay here and talk it out.

602
00:31:25.042 --> 00:31:28.044
Well, that's it. If you
want to try it, I'm ready.

603
00:31:28.045 --> 00:31:29.587
All right.
Let's do it the hard way.

604
00:31:29.588 --> 00:31:31.589
Okay, that sounds fair.
Everyone agreed?

605
00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:33.758
- Anyone doesn't agree?
- Fine, fine. Let's go.

606
00:31:33.759 --> 00:31:34.884
Here, pass these along.

607
00:31:34.885 --> 00:31:36.736
Is that the right time?
